-- ===================== LAZY SORCERER: AUTO GATE OPENING (LIVE-VERIFIED) =====================
-- Everything below was read out of the RUNNING client, not guessed:
--
--   Client.Controllers.QuestController.Handlers.TengenFog  (OnFocused, line 107)
--       InterfaceHandler_3 = InterfaceController:GetInterfaceHandler("Interact")
--       InterfaceHandler_3:OnInteracted(j.PrimaryPart.ProximityPrompt, function()
--           j.PrimaryPart.ProximityPrompt.Enabled = false     <-- definitive "this gate is open"
--           CommonLib.PlaySound(...TengenFog.Gate)
--           j.Model.Part.PointLight.Enabled = false
--           for _, e in j:QueryDescendants("ParticleEmitter, Beam") do e.Enabled = false end
--           u196 = u196 + 1
--           if u196 == u200 then u204:Fire() end              <-- FinishTengen, only after ALL gates
--       end)
--
--   Client.Controllers.InterfaceController.Interfaces.Interact  (line 30)
--       function u37.OnInteracted(self, prompt, callback)
--           return u37.Interacted:Connect(function(p) if p == prompt then callback() end end)
--       end
--       -- u37.Interacted is a sleitnick signal, and the ONLY thing that fires it is the custom
--       -- HUD "Options" component:  u9.Interacted:Connect(... u37.Interacted:Fire(p.ProximityPrompt))
--
-- >>> ROOT CAUSE OF "TELEPORTS BUT NEVER OPENS" <<<
-- The gates do NOT listen to ProximityPrompt.Triggered. Nothing in this game connects to it.
-- fireproximityprompt() raises Triggered, so the old code raised an event with zero listeners:
-- the walker teleported gate to gate, every prompt stayed Enabled = true, the quest counter
-- (u196) never moved and FinishTengen never fired. Pressing F did nothing either, because the
-- HUD Options component only forwards a keypress for the prompt it has currently SELECTED, and
-- only while the HUD is visible with no overlay open.
--
-- >>> THE CORRECT AUTOMATION PATH (verified live on this client) <<<
-- Open a gate by invoking the game's own registered interaction handler for that exact prompt:
-- walk Interact.Interacted._handlerListHead, match the connection whose closure upvalue IS this
-- gate's ProximityPrompt, and call its _fn(prompt). Live probe: prompt.Enabled went true -> false
-- and the gate opened. No keyboard, no VirtualInputManager, no F key, no prompt spam. The game
-- keeps its own counter honest, so it fires FinishTengen itself once the last gate is cleared.
-- There is NO per-gate remote in this flow; the only remote is QuestService.FinishTengen_Signal
-- (RemoteEvent, no arguments), kept strictly as a last-resort completion nudge.
--
-- >>> ROOT CAUSE OF "cannot resume dead coroutine" <<<
-- ReplicatedStorage.Packages.signal -> sleitnick_signal@2.0.3 uses the shared free-runner-thread
-- pattern with NO liveness check:
--       function Signal:Fire(...)
--           ... if not u0 then u0 = coroutine.create(runEventHandlerInFreeThread) end
--               task.spawn(u0, handler._fn, ...)
--       end
-- u0 is a single module-level thread shared by EVERY signal in the game. If a handler errors, or
-- the thread is cancelled/closed while it is parked, u0 keeps pointing at a DEAD thread and the
-- next :Fire() does task.spawn(deadThread, ...) -> "cannot resume dead coroutine". Firing that
-- signal in a tight retry loop (what the old gate code effectively did through the interact
-- system) is exactly how that thread gets killed.
-- The fix here is threefold and does not suppress anything:
--   1) we never use :Fire() for gates -- we invoke the matched handler in a FRESH thread we own,
--      so the shared runner thread is never touched by the hub at all;
--   2) NexoSignalHeal() repairs the library itself: if u0 is dead it is replaced with a fresh
--      coroutine.create(runEventHandlerInFreeThread) (or cleared so Fire rebuilds it), which
--      un-breaks the game's own interactions too;
--   3) the worker is a single supervised thread -- we only ever spawn a NEW function, never
--      resume, cancel or re-spawn an existing thread, and a generation token guarantees one
--      Auto Lazy Sorcerer loop can exist at a time.
LAZY_GATE_TRIES    = 8    -- interaction attempts per gate in one visit
LAZY_GATE_CHECK    = 0.12 -- pause between an attempt and re-checking whether it opened
LAZY_GATE_PASSES   = 6    -- full re-scan passes before the run gives up (anti-stuck ceiling)
LAZY_GATE_DEADLINE = 12   -- hard seconds budget per gate visit (anti-stuck)
NexoTengenSig      = nil
NexoInteractIF     = nil

-- The folder the game queries for gates.
